myths.Agent_1:
In the age before time had a name, the sky was a hollow, velvet void. The Great Fox, a creature of starlight and solitude, prowled the edges of the horizon, his tail brushing against the cosmic dust. He sought a companion to share the silence, but the stars were cold and distant, refusing to speak. Frustrated, the Fox began to run. He sprinted across the dome of the heavens, his fur igniting with the friction of his desperate speed.

As he galloped, his tail whipped through the shimmering layers of the atmosphere, scattering embers of emerald, violet, and crimson light. These were not mere sparks; they were the echoes of his longing, solidified into dancing ribbons of fire. The Great Fox eventually vanished into the deep dark, leaving behind his glowing trail as a reminder of his journey. Now, whenever the world grows too cold and the night feels hollow, the lights return. They are the spirit of the Fox, still racing across the night, illuminating the darkness for those who watch with open eyes. The people of the frost still look upward, whispering to the aurora, knowing that even in the vast nothingness, a flicker of warmth remains.

